            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"week-7-compliance-public-aggregate-data-vs-phi\">Week 7 \u2014 Compliance: public aggregate data vs. PHI</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Your layer is built from <strong>public, facility-level</strong> files: one row per hospital (or hospital x measure),\npublished by CMS and CDC for anyone to download. That is very different from <strong>patient-level</strong> data.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>The words, at the level this course uses them.</strong> Under HIPAA, <em>protected health information</em> (PHI) is\nindividually identifiable health information. HHS describes two ways to de-identify health information:\n<strong>Safe Harbor</strong> and <strong>Expert Determination</strong>. Look them up on the HHS de-identification guidance page\nbefore you rely on either; this notebook is a course check, not a legal review.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>The lesson:</strong> a hospital's name, address and phone number are <em>public facts about a facility</em>, not PHI.\nA patient's name, a date of birth, or a count small enough to point at a few people is a different matter.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Run the checklist below against your layer: pick one of the course examples, or upload the CSV you\nexported from your own notebook.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
